steps.body:
`container/heap` turns your own slice type into a heap once it has five methods: `Len`, `Less`, and `Swap`, plus `Push` and `Pop` with pointer receivers, since they change the slice's length. `Less(i, j)` says whether element i belongs above element j, so for structs it compares the main key and then a tie-break:

```go harness
{{code}}

h := &TaskHeap{{"b", 1}, {"a", 1}, {"c", 2}}
heap.Init(h)
fmt.Println(heap.Pop(h).(Task).Name)
```

```go
type Task struct {
	Name     string
	Priority int
}

type TaskHeap []Task

func (h TaskHeap) Len() int      { return len(h) }
func (h TaskHeap) Swap(i, j int) { h[i], h[j] = h[j], h[i] }
func (h TaskHeap) Less(i, j int) bool {
	if h[i].Priority != h[j].Priority {
		return h[i].Priority > h[j].Priority // the highest priority on top
	}
	return h[i].Name < h[j].Name // ties: A to Z
}
func (h *TaskHeap) Push(x interface{}) { *h = append(*h, x.(Task)) }
func (h *TaskHeap) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *h
	x := old[len(old)-1]
	*h = old[:len(old)-1]
	return x
}
```

The tie-break makes the order complete. Without it, two tasks with the same priority could come out in either order, and a heap doesn't keep input order the way a stable sort does.

Then go through the package's functions, never your own `Push` and `Pop`, which only append and cut the end:

