Merging k sorted lists means repeatedly taking the smallest value at the front of any list. A heap holds one front value per list, so each pick costs O(log k), and merging N values in all takes O(N log k).

Each heap entry remembers where its value came from, so you know which list moves next:

```go harness
{{code}}

h := &EntryHeap{{5, 0, 0}, {2, 1, 0}}
heap.Init(h)
fmt.Println(heap.Pop(h).(Entry))
```

```go
// Entry is one value waiting in the heap, with where it came from.
type Entry struct {
	Value, List, Index int
}

type EntryHeap []Entry

func (h EntryHeap) Len() int            { return len(h) }
func (h EntryHeap) Less(i, j int) bool  { return h[i].Value < h[j].Value }
func (h EntryHeap) Swap(i, j int)       { h[i], h[j] = h[j], h[i] }
func (h *EntryHeap) Push(x interface{}) { *h = append(*h, x.(Entry)) }
func (h *EntryHeap) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *h
	x := old[len(old)-1]
	*h = old[:len(old)-1]
	return x
}
```

Start with the first value of every list that isn't empty. Then pop the smallest, record it, and push the next value from the **same** list, if it has one:

```go harness
