steps.body:
The median of numbers that keep arriving needs the middle at every moment. Two heaps split the numbers in half:

- `low`, a **max-heap**, holds the smaller half, so its top is the biggest of the small numbers.
- `high`, a **min-heap**, holds the larger half, so its top is the smallest of the big numbers.

Go 1.16 has no generics, so the two halves need two types, `MaxHeap` and `MinHeap`, with the same methods except for `Less`. To add a number, push it into `low`, move `low`'s top over to `high`, and if `high` is now bigger, move its top back. `low` keeps the extra number when the count is odd:

```go harness
type MinHeap []int

func (h MinHeap) Len() int            { return len(h) }
func (h MinHeap) Less(i, j int) bool  { return h[i] < h[j] }
func (h MinHeap) Swap(i, j int)       { h[i], h[j] = h[j], h[i] }
func (h *MinHeap) Push(x interface{}) { *h = append(*h, x.(int)) }
func (h *MinHeap) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *h
	x := old[len(old)-1]
	*h = old[:len(old)-1]
	return x
}

type MaxHeap []int

func (h MaxHeap) Len() int            { return len(h) }
func (h MaxHeap) Less(i, j int) bool  { return h[i] > h[j] }
func (h MaxHeap) Swap(i, j int)       { h[i], h[j] = h[j], h[i] }
func (h *MaxHeap) Push(x interface{}) { *h = append(*h, x.(int)) }
func (h *MaxHeap) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *h
	x := old[len(old)-1]
	*h = old[:len(old)-1]
	return x
}

{{code}}
```

```go
low, high := &MaxHeap{}, &MinHeap{}
for _, x := range []int{5, 15, 1, 3} {
	heap.Push(low, x)
	heap.Push(high, heap.Pop(low)) // the biggest small number moves up
	if high.Len() > low.Len() {
		heap.Push(low, heap.Pop(high)) // low keeps the extra one
	}
	fmt.Println((*low)[0], low.Len(), high.Len())
}
```

Moving through `low` first keeps every number in `low` at most every number in `high`. Then the median is `(*low)[0]` for an odd count, and the average of the two tops for an even one. `heap.Pop` returns an `interface{}` and `heap.Push` takes one, so a popped value can go straight into the other heap. Each new number costs O(log n).

